# Press an unrecognised key in the Skill Manager and see whether the ranks
# survive it. This is the oracle for inc-0o0r.
#
#   tools/headless.sh tools/keys/skill-manager-reset.keys 5
#
# The offending keys are HOME and END, and they are not an odd choice of
# keyboard: they are what the left stick sends. poll_gamepad maps the eight
# octants onto the numeric keypad (src/Wlibtcod.cpp:729-731), so UP-LEFT
# arrives as KP7 -> KY_HOME -> KY_CMD_NORTHWEST and DOWN-LEFT as KP1 ->
# KY_END -> KY_CMD_SOUTHWEST (src/Tables.cpp:4683 and :4685). Neither had a
# case in TextTerm::SkillManager's switch, so both fell into default:, which
# was also the exit path -- and during character generation the exit path
# runs Reset and throws every allocation away. The two right-hand diagonals
# do have cases (they scroll the description) and were always harmless, which
# is why Brian saw the left diagonals fire and the right ones behave.
#
# The character generation prefix is chargen.keys' own, copied rather than
# included because this script must stop inside the first Skill Manager and
# chargen.keys walks straight through it. Keep the two in step.
#
# Race, subrace, class and alignment use @choose to select by name. Use a
# fixed seed: attribute sets have no names, and rolls decide the feats.
# Start-menu and yes/no answers stay positional; arrow bursts move the cursor
# and allocate ranks rather than answer named choices.
#
# Four screen dumps. The comparison between them is the whole test:
# This header used to say three screen dumps; corrected 2026-09-12.
#
#   before      three skills carrying ranks, cursor on the third.
#   after-end   after one END. It MUST equal "before" -- END is not a key
#               this screen knows, so it MUST do nothing at all.
#   after-home  after one HOME. Same requirement.
#   after-esc-no  after ESC then n. The ranks MUST remain allocated.
#
# Before the fix, both "after" screens show an empty allocation column, a
# refilled pool and the cursor back on the first row. See
# tools/check_skill_manager_reset.sh.

@expect "Initial Choices"
a       # Initial Choices: [a] Create a New Character; not a screen @choose reads.
@expect "Alter the character generation options?"
n       # Alter the character generation options? -- no; a yes/no question.
@expect "Explore Mode. Continue?"
y       # Continue in Explore Mode? Yes: a yes/no question.
@choose "Orc"
@choose "Orc (Standard)"
@choose "Barbarian"
@expect "Which of the five attribute sets"
a       # Attributes: the first of the five offered sets; the sets have no names.
@choose "Neutral"
@expect "Choose a feat"
a       # First feat; the attribute rolls decide which feats are offered.
@expect "Choose a feat"
a       # Second feat; the attribute rolls decide which feats are offered.

# The Skill Manager. Spend three ranks on each of the first three skills and
# stop, leaving the pool non-empty -- an empty pool takes the other half of
# the defect (a silent exit) and this run is measuring the wipe.
@expect "Skill Manager"
# Arrows allocate ranks and move between skills.
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3
@dump:before

END                 # left stick DOWN-LEFT
@dump:after-end
HOME                # left stick UP-LEFT
@dump:after-home

# ESC is the other half of the report: the footer calls it Abort, and it used
# to abort by wiping without asking. It asks now, so declining MUST leave the
# ranks alone. The screen is redrawn from Recount rather than left untouched,
# so this dump is checked on its contents and not byte-for-byte.
ESC n
@dump:after-esc-no

# Finish. UP*40 puts the cursor back on the first row from wherever the run
# left it, so the same burst empties the pool in a fixed build and in a
# broken one. Character generation will not let go until the pool is empty.
UP*40
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
ENTER y

@expect "Do you wish to worship"
y       # Do you wish to worship <god>? -- yes; a yes/no question.

@expect "Your personality archetype"
# Arrows move the selection on the personality archetype menu.
# Personality affects only the sheet banner, so its selection is not pinned.

RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
ENTER y

@quit
